both findings confirms it). This *doesn't* pick a winner — it's the reason the mistake is dangerous. There is no import graph and no compiler check to tell you that you edited the dead file.\n\n## If you added `deletedAt` to `user-schema 2.ts` instead\n\nNothing would break, and that's the whole problem:\n\n- **No error, no signal.** Zero importers, and zod strips unknown keys by default, so if anything ever does wire up the canonical schema, `deletedAt` is silently dropped from every parsed record. The write path keeps working; soft-deleted users just read back as live ones.\n- **You'd deepen the drift you were supposed to leave alone.** `crime_00001` currently reports one disagreement between the two schemas (`role`). Adding a field to the stale copy makes it two, and inverts the direction of the evidence — the copy would now hold a field the canonical lacks, so the \"which one is newer?\" question stops having a clean answer.\n- **You'd disguise the dead file.** Recent `git blame` activity on `user-schema 2.ts` is the strongest thing a future reader has to go on. Editing it makes the conflict copy look like the live schema and the real one look abandoned — the next agent inherits a harder version of this exact decision.\n- **It's committed.** `git ls-files` lists `src/repo/user-schema 2.ts`, so this replicates to every clone; it isn't a local artifact someone will clean up.\n\n## The edit (canonical file)\n\n```diff\n--- a/src/repo/user-schema.ts\n+++ b/src/repo/user-schema.ts\n@@ -7,4 +7,5 @@ export const UserSchema = z.object({\n   role: z.enum([\"admin\", \"member\", \"owner\"]),\n   plan: z.enum([\"free\", \"pro\", \"enterprise\"]),\n   createdAt: z.string(),\n+  deletedAt: z.string().optional(),\n });\n```\n\n`z.string()` to match the existing `createdAt` treatment; `.optional()` because a live user has no deletion timestamp. Separately worth deciding — not doing silently — whether `src/contracts/user.ts` needs `deletedAt` too: if soft-deleted users are ever